WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:01.634
New tonight, a lawyer for

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.369
Jean Carroll

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:04.471
saying the writer may sue Trump again

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:05.071
for comments

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:06.339
he made during last night's

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:07.574
CNN town hall,

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:08.942
mocking her claim that he raped her

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:09.909
in a Manhattan Department

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:12.979
store in the mid 1990s.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:16.182
I never met this woman.

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:19.052
I never saw this woman, this woman said.

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:19.719
I met her

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:21.521
at the front door of Bergdorf Goodman,

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:22.789
which I rarely go into, other

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:24.190
than for a couple of charities.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.361
This is a fake story, made up story

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:30.964
attorney for Carroll

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:32.032
telling The New York Times.

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:33.800
Everything's on the table, obviously,

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.769
and we have to give serious consideration

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:37.437
to suing Trump again.

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:38.338
It comes as Trump

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:39.672
officially filed his appeal

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.908
and the $5 million jury decision

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:44.177
that found that he sexually abused

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:45.979
and defamed Carroll.

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:47.514
OUTFRONT now, Ryan Goodman, co

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:49.416
editor in chief of Just Security.

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:49.983
Also former

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:50.583
special counsel

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:51.818
of the Defense Department.

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:53.887
So, Ryan, let's start there.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:54.954
These these comments

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:56.489
we've just played from Trump,

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:58.291
they match almost identically

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.659
with his social media posts

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:02.695
on October 2022, almost identically.

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:04.097
But that is very significant

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:05.999
because that post is a post

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:08.034
that the jury found to be defamatory

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:09.502
in this case. Quote, I don't know.

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:11.071
This woman have no idea who she is.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:13.339
She completely made up a story.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:15.475
So do you think she could win

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:17.177
another defamation case?

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:18.611
Absolutely.

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:19.979
Because it is a kind of a replica

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:21.181
of what he said before.

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:22.182
A jury has already found

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:24.818
unanimously that that was defamatory.

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:25.952
And, in fact,

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.620
she could actually walk into the court

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:28.788
and say

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:30.123
part of this has already been decided

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:31.524
between these two parties.

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:31.958
There's already

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:33.193
a unanimous verdict

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:34.861
that I was telling the truth

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:36.196
about the sexual assault.

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:36.830
And what he did

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.531
was maliciously defaming me.

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:40.500
And here he has done it again. Right.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.835
So she could absolutely do it now.

00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:43.336
I guess the big question is, will she

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:44.404
I mean, she already has a victory.

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:46.539
She's got $5 million.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:46.906
You know, it's

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:47.974
a very personal choice

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:48.541
as to whether or not

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:49.242
she wants to do it

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:51.511
and put herself through another trial.

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:52.946
But she might be able

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:54.147
to convince the judge that,

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:55.381
as I mentioned, in a certain sense,

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.149
some of these issues

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:57.016
have already been decided.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.651
So she does not have to

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:00.520
go through the rigor again

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.455
of going through what happened to her

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:03.990
that will already be established.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:04.524
And then she can

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:06.126
just go to the question of defamation,

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:07.127
not to the question of whether or not

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.495
there was a sexual assault.

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:09.996
Okay. So take that part away.

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:10.330
All right.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.564
Which could be very significant.

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:12.232
Now, you're

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:13.399
pointing to a few other comments

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:13.800
last night

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.668
that could impact the two DOJ

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:16.603
criminal cases

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:17.770
in which we are still awaiting

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.806
potential indictment from the DOJ.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:22.208
So let's start with January six.

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:23.843
Caitlin asked Trump about the danger

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:24.878
that was posed to his

00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:26.980
then Vice President, Mike Pence.

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:28.047
Let me play that exchange.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:31.317
The person who was at the

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.153
Capitol that day, as you know,

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:35.155
was your vice president, Mike Pence,

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:37.223
who says that you endangered his life

00:02:37.524 --> 00:02:38.391
on that day.

00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:39.893
I don't think he was in any danger.

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:40.126
Mr.

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:40.860
President,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.962
do you feel that you owe him an apology?

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:44.797
No, because he did something wrong.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:45.865
He should have put the votes

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:47.567
back to the state legislatures.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:48.001
And I think

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:50.670
we would have had a different outcome

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.539
You find that incriminating.

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:53.406
Explain.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.942
It's very similar to the testimony

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:56.309
that was given

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:57.410
to the January six committee

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.478
where he says

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:00.780
Pence deserved it in the sense

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.415
that he was put in danger.

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:03.583
And then the answer is no.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:04.984
But he is responsible

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:07.320
for having put himself in danger by not

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.855
decertifying or trying to interfere

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:10.590
with the certification of the election.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:11.558
He's seeing it similarly.

00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:12.058
And also

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:13.426
his lawyer says that to Pence's

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:15.361
lawyer in the midst of the attack,

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.564
Eastman says to Greg Jacob,

00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:19.666
No you all are responsible for this,

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:21.434
because if your boss, Pence,

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:22.135
had acted differently,

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:23.403
there wouldn't be this violence

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:25.205
that's very incriminating.

00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:26.706
And it also goes with him

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:27.941
suggesting that he would pardon

00:03:27.941 --> 00:03:28.474
some of these people,

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:30.043
even up to the Proud Boys.

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:31.444
It's an endorsement of the violence

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:32.011
in a certain sense.

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.479
And I think that would be really

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:34.647
important evidence

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:37.083
that, in fact, that the DOJ would use

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:37.684
before a jury

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:38.651
if they did bring it to that.

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.053
All right. So that is significant.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:41.287
There's also the Mar a Lago

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:42.689
classified documents case

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:44.557
that you think is front and center here.

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:46.726
Let me play the exchange

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:47.961
over the classified documents

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:51.264
Did you ever show those

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:53.166
classified documents to anyone?

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:53.833
Not really.

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:55.101
I would have the right to.

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:57.737
By the way, they were demystified after.

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.072
Not that I can think of.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:00.273
Let me just tell you,

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:01.107
I have the absolute

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:04.143
right to do whatever I want with them.

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:05.578
Not really.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:08.281
And not that I can think of.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:11.184
So that's hedging on this

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:12.285
very important question.

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:13.653
Did you share it with anybody else?

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:15.788
If he shared that those documents

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:16.723
with anybody else?

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:17.590
It puts the case

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:18.691
in a very different category

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.093
under the Espionage Act

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:21.561
and it ratchets up the likelihood

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:22.395
that he'll be indicted,

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:23.229
because that's a very different

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:23.830
kind of a case

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.665
if you share it with other people.

00:04:25.665 --> 00:04:26.899
The fact that he's hedging like that,

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:27.900
I'm sure

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.602
everybody inside of Jake Smith's,

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:30.436
the special counsel's

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.372
office, their ears perked up

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:33.640
because he's not denying

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.307
that he did that.

00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:35.308
It sounds like there's

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:36.843
something really there.

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:37.310
Plus, he's

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:38.678
also saying that he was aware

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:40.013
that the documents were removed

00:04:40.013 --> 00:04:40.980
from the White House.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:41.748
That's another piece

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:43.016
that he said last night.

00:04:43.016 --> 00:04:44.550
That is really important to the case.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:44.951
All right.

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:45.451
Well, of course,

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:46.252
as we await

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.221
those those possible indictments,

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:49.422
whether there would be one

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:50.923
in January six as well,

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:51.391
Mar a Lago,

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:53.359
whether they come together or not.

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:53.793
We await.

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:55.395
All right, Ryan, thank you very much.

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:56.129
And I want to go now

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:57.230
to a Republican congressman

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.765
from New York, Mike Lawler.

00:04:58.765 --> 00:05:00.166
He has said the GOP needs

00:05:00.166 --> 00:05:01.200
to move on from Trump.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.735
And Congressman, I appreciate your time.

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:03.736
So, you know,

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:05.838
you heard Ryan there explaining why

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:07.740
Trump may have put himself

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.242
in even more legal jeopardy

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:10.176
with those comments

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:11.277
that we were just playing

00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:12.478
about the classified documents

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.814
case about Pence and January six.

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:17.183
But of course, the other part of this

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:18.217
is that the people in the room

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:20.086
were applauding Trump as he said this.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.622
What's your feeling about that?

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:22.955
Does it make

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:25.725
you want to move on from Trump even more?

00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:28.995
Well, I can't speak to the CNN town hall

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:30.029
or how,

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.532
you know, the audience was selected

00:05:32.899 --> 00:05:34.334
or the topics discussed.

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:35.234
I mean, that's something

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:36.002
obviously you guys

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:37.270
can speak to better than I.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.771
But, you know,

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:41.207
look, I've said repeatedly that

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:41.441
you know,

00:05:41.441 --> 00:05:43.543
I think the party needs to move in

00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:46.546
in a new direction that I think

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:48.481
whoever the nominee is needs

00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:50.683
to be focused on the future.

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:51.584
And the challenges

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:53.753
facing the American people right now

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:55.021
in Congress,

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:55.188
we're

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:56.823
focused on the debt ceiling

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:58.958
and the migrant crisis

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:01.060
impacting our nation.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.562
And, you know,

00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:03.863
when I met with the president

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:05.231
yesterday in my district,

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:06.599
that is what I talked about.

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:09.035
That was my focus on the issues

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:11.070
that we're dealing with.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:11.971
And so,

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:12.271
you know,

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:15.041
I'm not interested in relitigating 2020.

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:16.809
I've said repeatedly, Joe Biden

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:18.511
won the election.

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:20.480
It's time to move on from that.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:21.614
So so and I'm going to ask you

00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:23.116
both about debt and immigration.

00:06:23.116 --> 00:06:23.783
But but first,

00:06:23.783 --> 00:06:25.017
I do want to ask you one other thing,

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:26.586
because what matters here

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:28.287
at this moment in time,

00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:29.655
whatever the predicate is,

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:31.924
is what other Republicans

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:33.626
are going to do.

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:34.794
You know, you're talking about yourself,

00:06:34.794 --> 00:06:36.062
but there are Republicans

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:36.629
who have been strong

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:38.264
supporters of Trump in the past

00:06:38.264 --> 00:06:39.265
who are breaking with him

00:06:39.265 --> 00:06:41.000
over some of his remarks last night.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.068
And I wanted to play two of them.

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:42.869
They may surprise you.

00:06:42.869 --> 00:06:44.370
Senator Hawley is one of them.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.406
Senator Tillis is another one.

00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:47.507
They're weighing in

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:47.940
on the fact

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.375
that Trump promised

00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:52.145
yet again pardons for January 6th riders.

00:06:52.311 --> 00:06:52.779
Here they are

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:55.948
Anybody who

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:59.085
crossed in to the

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:00.119
end of the Capitol,

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:01.287
under the circumstances

00:07:01.287 --> 00:07:02.889
that I witnessed firsthand,

00:07:02.889 --> 00:07:04.457
it's hard for me to have a positive

00:07:04.457 --> 00:07:06.392
predisposition towards them.

00:07:06.392 --> 00:07:07.727
But just generally, I disagree.

00:07:07.727 --> 00:07:08.394
But if you're asking me, do

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:08.828
I think you should

00:07:08.828 --> 00:07:10.396
pardon people who engage in, like,

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:12.198
rioting behavior? No, I don't.

00:07:12.198 --> 00:07:12.632
And

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:14.834
now of course,

00:07:14.834 --> 00:07:16.068
Senator Holly, as you're well aware,

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:17.136
Congressman voted to overturn

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:17.703
the election.

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:18.571
He had that famous,

00:07:18.571 --> 00:07:18.771
you know,

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:19.872
fist bump to the pro-Trump

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:21.073
crowd on January six

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:23.042
before it stormed the Capitol.

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:24.076
But neither of those senators

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:24.977
actually went so far

00:07:24.977 --> 00:07:26.212
as to say they would not support Trump

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:27.313
if it is a nominee.

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:28.681
They're not saying, let's move it.

00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:30.183
They're not saying what you're saying.

00:07:30.183 --> 00:07:31.951
Right. Loudly and clearly.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:33.252
So what do you read into this?

00:07:33.252 --> 00:07:34.520
Do you think this is the beginning

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:35.688
of real cracks in Trump's

00:07:35.688 --> 00:07:36.722
core support or not?

00:07:38.024 --> 00:07:40.259
Well, look, I can't speak to others

00:07:40.293 --> 00:07:41.260
and what they think

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.161
or how they're going

00:07:42.161 --> 00:07:45.198
to act during this primary process.

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:46.999
What I can say

00:07:46.999 --> 00:07:49.035
is this with respect to January six,

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:51.137
I was the first state legislator

00:07:51.137 --> 00:07:53.306
in New York to condemn it.

00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:56.108
I don't care, frankly, who it is.

00:07:56.342 --> 00:07:58.478
Anybody who stormed the Capitol

00:07:58.478 --> 00:07:59.779
should be held responsible

00:07:59.779 --> 00:08:01.614
for their own conduct,

00:08:01.614 --> 00:08:02.415
just like anybody

00:08:02.415 --> 00:08:03.049
who stormed

00:08:03.049 --> 00:08:05.485
the federal courthouse in Oregon

00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:06.652
or anybody

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:07.687
who burned a police

00:08:07.687 --> 00:08:08.988
precinct to the ground.

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:11.324
When you commit acts of violence,

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:13.893
you should be held liable.

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:14.760
Period.

00:08:15.094 --> 00:08:18.631
And as far as I'm concerned, you know,

00:08:19.732 --> 00:08:20.967
that's the bottom line on this.

